\section{Kinship coefficient (\code{"kinship"})}{

The kinship coefficient \eqn{\phi_{ij}}{phi[i,j]} (Malécot 1948, who called
it the \emph{coefficient de parenté}; it is also known as the coefficient of
coancestry) is the probability that an allele drawn at
random from \eqn{i} and an allele drawn at random from \eqn{j}, at the same
autosomal locus, are identical by descent, i.e. copies of a single allele of
a common ancestor. Being a probability, it lies between 0 and 1. This is
what \code{\link[kinship2:kinship]{kinship2::kinship()}} returns (Sinnwell et al. 2014).

Two related quantities follow. The inbreeding coefficient \eqn{F_i}{F[i]},
introduced by Wright (1922) as a correlation between the gametes that unite
to form \eqn{i} and reinterpreted by Malécot (1948) as a probability, is the
probability that the two alleles of \eqn{i} are identical by descent; it
equals the kinship coefficient between the parents of \eqn{i}. The kinship of an individual with itself is
\eqn{\phi_{ii} = (1 + F_i)/2}{phi[i,i] = (1 + F[i]) / 2}: when two alleles
are drawn from \eqn{i} with replacement, the same allele is drawn twice with
probability 1/2, and otherwise the two alleles are identical by descent with
probability \eqn{F_i}{F[i]}.
}

\section{Additive relationship (\code{"additive"})}{

The additive relationship \eqn{A_{ij} = 2\phi_{ij}}{A[i,j] = 2 phi[i,j]} is
what animal and plant breeders call the numerator relationship matrix
(Henderson 1976), computed here with the tabular method (Emik and Terrill
1949). It is not a probability but an expected count: \eqn{A_{ij}}{A[i,j]}
is the expected number of the two alleles of \eqn{j} that are identical by
descent with an allele drawn at random from \eqn{i}, which is why it can
exceed 1 (see the section Range of values). For example, two
offspring obtained by selfing an individual that was itself obtained by
selfing have an additive relationship of 1.5 (and a kinship of 0.75).

Its main use is quantitative genetics: under an additive model, the
covariance between the breeding values of \eqn{i} and \eqn{j} is
\eqn{A_{ij} \sigma^2_A}{A[i,j] * sigma2_A}, where \eqn{\sigma^2_A}{sigma2_A}
is the additive genetic variance of the base population (Lynch and Walsh
1998, chapter 7). The diagonal, \eqn{1 + F_i}{1 + F[i]}, shows that the
breeding values of inbred individuals vary more, since inbred individuals
are more homozygous. This matrix is the one used in animal models and to
estimate heritability.
}

\section{Wright's coefficient of relationship (\code{"wright"})}{

Wright (1922) defined the coefficient of relationship as the correlation
between the genetic values of two individuals, which is obtained by dividing
the covariance \eqn{A_{ij}}{A[i,j]} by the product of the two standard
deviations:
\deqn{r_{ij} = \frac{A_{ij}}{\sqrt{A_{ii} A_{jj}}} = \frac{2\phi_{ij}}{\sqrt{(1 + F_i)(1 + F_j)}}}{r[i,j] = A[i,j] / sqrt(A[i,i] * A[j,j]) = 2 phi[i,j] / sqrt((1 + F[i]) * (1 + F[j]))}
This is what \code{\link[stats:cor]{stats::cov2cor()}} computes from the additive relationship
matrix. Being a correlation, it lies between 0 and 1 (the Cauchy-Schwarz
inequality guarantees that
\eqn{A_{ij} \le \sqrt{A_{ii} A_{jj}}}{A[i,j] <= sqrt(A[i,i] * A[j,j])}), and
every individual has a relationship of 1 with itself.

Its interpretation follows: it measures how similar two individuals are
compared with how similar an individual is to itself. A value of 1 means
that two individuals are \strong{as similar as an individual is to itself}, as
with members of a line selfed for many generations; a value of 0.5 means
that they are half as similar. It does not remove the effect of inbreeding
on resemblance, it measures resemblance against that reference, which is
why it stays between 0 and 1 whatever the inbreeding. In the selfing example
above, the two offspring have \eqn{A = 1.5} but
\eqn{r = 1.5 / 1.75 \approx 0.86}{r = 1.5 / 1.75 = 0.86}: they are 86
percent as similar to each other as each of them is to itself.
}

\section{Hamilton's regression coefficient of relatedness (\code{"hamilton"})}{

Hamilton (1964a, b) used Wright's coefficient of relationship in the first
formulation of inclusive fitness. He later showed that with inbreeding the
relevant measure for kin selection, the \eqn{r} of Hamilton's rule
\eqn{rb > c}, is a regression coefficient rather than a correlation
(Hamilton 1972; Michod and Hamilton 1980): the regression of the genetic
value of the recipient of a behaviour on that of the actor,
\deqn{b_{ij} = \frac{\mathrm{Cov}(g_i, g_j)}{\mathrm{Var}(g_i)} = \frac{A_{ij}}{A_{ii}} = \frac{2\phi_{ij}}{1 + F_i}}{b[i,j] = Cov(g[i], g[j]) / Var(g[i]) = A[i,j] / A[i,i] = 2 phi[i,j] / (1 + F[i])}
where \eqn{i} is the actor and \eqn{j} the recipient. In the matrix
returned, \strong{rows are actors and columns are recipients}: \code{R[i, j]} is the
relatedness of recipient \code{j} from the point of view of actor \code{i}. The
matrix is not symmetric when individuals differ in their inbreeding
coefficients. Its values lie between 0 and 1 (see the section Range of values), and
all individuals have a relatedness of 1 with themselves. Its interpretation
is similar to that of Wright's coefficient, but from the point of view of
the actor only: \code{R[i, j] = 1} means that, as far as the genes of the actor
\code{i} are concerned, the recipient \code{j} is as similar to \code{i} as \code{i} is to
itself.
}

\section{Range of values}{

The ranges given in the second table all follow from one property: in a
pedigree, nobody is more related to another individual than to itself,
\deqn{A_{ij} \le \min(A_{ii}, A_{jj})}{A[i,j] <= min(A[i,i], A[j,j])}
Consequently:
\itemize{
\item The off-diagonal values of the additive relationship are at most 2 and
those of the kinship at most 1, while Wright's coefficient (the additive
relationship divided by the geometric mean of the two diagonal values)
and Hamilton's coefficient (divided by the diagonal value of the actor)
are at most 1.
\item Without inbreeding, every diagonal value of the additive relationship is
1, so the additive relationship between two individuals is then at most
1, the value for identical twins or clones.
\item Values of the additive relationship above 1 therefore only occur with
inbreeding. For example, after \code{n} generations of selfing the inbreeding
coefficient is \code{1 - (1/2)^n}, so the diagonal is 1.5, 1.75, 1.875, ...
The maximum of 2 (a kinship of 1) is approached but never reached, since
founders are assumed not to be inbred.
}

Here is why the property holds. Take two individuals, and call \eqn{i} the one that
is not an ancestor of the other, \eqn{j}; let \eqn{m} and \eqn{d} be the
parents of \eqn{i}, so that
\eqn{A_{ij} = (A_{mj} + A_{dj})/2}{A[i,j] = (A[m,j] + A[d,j]) / 2} and
\eqn{A_{ii} = 1 + A_{md}/2}{A[i,i] = 1 + A[m,d] / 2}.
\itemize{
\item Draw one allele at random from each of \eqn{j}, \eqn{m} and \eqn{d}. If
the allele of \eqn{j} is identical by descent both with that of \eqn{m}
and with that of \eqn{d}, then these two are identical by descent with
each other. In terms of probabilities,
\eqn{\phi_{mj} + \phi_{dj} \le 1 + \phi_{md}}{phi[m,j] + phi[d,j] <= 1 + phi[m,d]},
i.e. \eqn{A_{mj} + A_{dj} \le 2 + A_{md}}{A[m,j] + A[d,j] <= 2 + A[m,d]},
which is \eqn{A_{ij} \le A_{ii}}{A[i,j] <= A[i,i]}.
\item \eqn{A_{ij}}{A[i,j]} is the mean of \eqn{A_{mj}}{A[m,j]} and
\eqn{A_{dj}}{A[d,j]}. If neither exceeds \eqn{A_{jj}}{A[j,j]}, their
mean does not either. As \eqn{m} and \eqn{d} come before \eqn{i} in the
pedigree, the result follows step by step from the founders, which are
related to nobody but themselves.
}

An unknown parent counts as a founder that appears nowhere else in the
pedigree, so the same reasoning applies.
}

\section{Assumptions}{

Whatever the measure, founders (individuals with unknown parents) are
assumed to be unrelated to each other and not inbred, an unknown parent is
treated as unrelated to everybody, and only autosomal loci are considered.
All values are therefore relative to the founders of the pedigree.
}

\examples{
## G is a founder; P is obtained by selfing G; O1 and O2 by selfing P
ped <- data.frame(id  = c("G", "P", "O1", "O2"),
                  mom = c(NA, "G", "P", "P"),
                  dad = c(NA, "G", "P", "P"))
fastrel_ped(ped)                       # additive relationship, the default
fastrel_ped(ped, metric = "kinship")   # probabilities
fastrel_ped(ped, metric = "wright")    # correlations
fastrel_ped(ped, metric = "hamilton")  # regressions: row = actor

## the conversions from the additive relationship matrix
A <- fastrel_ped(ped)
all.equal(fastrel_ped(ped, metric = "kinship"), A / 2)
all.equal(fastrel_ped(ped, metric = "wright"), cov2cor(A))
all.equal(fastrel_ped(ped, metric = "hamilton"), A / diag(A))

## without inbreeding, all but the kinship are the same
ped2 <- data.frame(id = c("a", "b", "c", "d"), mom = c(NA, NA, "a", "a"),
                   dad = c(NA, NA, "b", "b"))
all.equal(fastrel_ped(ped2), fastrel_ped(ped2, metric = "wright"))
all.equal(fastrel_ped(ped2), fastrel_ped(ped2, metric = "hamilton"))
}
